{"id": "q0173", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "balanced", "tier": "balanced", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 2096371714, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "0c0eb5f5089b4570f4b5f593318ef7c0c5f8ba06a58bdaf93a6686b9afa1d86e", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.7972545623779297, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 4, "query": "A defendant and his college roommate went home together for the holiday vacation. The defendant was to go back to college by himself. The day that the defendant was to drive back to college, his roommate asked him if he would deliver a package to one of their classmates. The defendant agreed, and the roommate gave him a small package, which the defendant placed in his glove compartment. While driving back to school, the defendant was stopped for speeding. When the state trooper asked the defendant for his license and registration, he reached into the glove compartment. As he did, the package that his roommate had given him fell onto the floor. When it hit the floor, the box broke open and a plastic envelope containing a white substance slid out. Seeing that the substance appeared to be cocaine, the state trooper arrested the defendant for possession of illegal narcotics under a state statute making it a felony to willfully possess a controlled substance. The package did, in fact, contain cocaine. If the defendant is later brought to trial for this charge, his best defense is that\n\nA. the package didn't belong to him.\nB. the cocaine was illegally seized.\nC. the trooper didn't have a search warrant.\nD. the defendant didn't know what was in the package.\nE. the roommate didn't tell him he was carrying illegal narcotics.\nF. the roommate should be the one charged, not him.\nG. the substance was not tested to confirm it was cocaine.\nH. the defendant didn't willingly possess the substance.\nI. the cocaine was not in a recognizable form.\nJ. the defendant was not speeding.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0173.wav", "input_sha256": "a375462cd0ee9486b7ec95089c6abfd66df71d0ed6dd6c86adeb149a655dce8c", "input_samples": 1611400, "input_s": 100.713, "n_real_chunks": 101, "score": 0.5909, "act": 0.9996, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Well, let's go through these options. A: The package didn' t belong to him. While that might be true, possession laws often focus on having control over the substance regardless of ownership. B: The cocaine was illegally seized. That could be a valid defense if the trooper exceeded legal authority during the stop. C: The trooper didn't have a search warrant. This is tricky because traffic stops typically allow officers to make reasonable observations without warrants, but if they escalate into a full search without cause, it could be an issue. D: The defendant didn't know what was in the package. Lack of knowledge doesn't always excuse possession, especially if he had reason to suspect or was asked about it. E: The roommate didn' t tell him he was carrying illegal narcotics. This points to a potential lack of intent or awareness on his part, which might matter legally. F: The room mate should be the one charged, not him. That shifts responsibility, but unless he knew and aided in its transport, it may not fully absolve him. G: The substance wasn' t tested to confirm it was cocaine. If the evidence isn't properly authenticated, that could undermine the prosecution's case. H: The co caine was not willingly possessed. Since he was transporting it under duress or mistake, this might challenge the willfulness required for the felony charge. I: The defendant was not speeding with the correct option letter, and its content. This seems like a misstatement; likely the intended point was about whether he was aware of the substance. J: The defendant did n't willingly possess the substance. Given he was asked to carry it and placed it in his car, there's likely enough willful action to sustain the charge. After reviewing all these, the strongest defense would probably be that the trooper did not have a search warrant or that the substance", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 76, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 60, "feed_lag_max_ms": 1783, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 17754, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0173_balanced_local.wav", "sha256": "49462b7c7743c0eb181a4ea44014beaaebc6f6abdf808d0eae0473f79505c7d7", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 59.0}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.084, 1.15, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.085, 2.153, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.087, 3.154, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 4.0, 4.088, 4.156, 1, 0, 0], [4, 1, 5.0, 5.088, 5.159, 1, 0, 0], [5, 1, 6.0, 6.088, 6.159, 1, 0, 0], [6, 1, 7.0, 7.087, 7.156, 1, 0, 0], [7, 1, 8.0, 8.089, 8.159, 1, 0, 0], [8, 1, 9.0, 9.09, 9.163, 1, 0, 0], [9, 1, 10.0, 10.085, 10.152, 1, 0, 0], [10, 1, 11.0, 11.09, 11.159, 1, 0, 0], [11, 1, 12.0, 12.085, 12.152, 1, 0, 0], [12, 1, 13.0, 13.09, 13.161, 1, 0, 0], [13, 1, 14.0, 14.089, 14.16, 1, 0, 0], [14, 1, 15.0, 15.093, 15.163, 1, 0, 0], [15, 1, 16.0, 16.093, 16.166, 1, 0, 0], [16, 1, 17.0, 17.091, 17.162, 1, 0, 0], [17, 1, 18.0, 18.09, 18.162, 1, 0, 0], [18, 1, 19.0, 19.091, 19.16, 1, 0, 0], [19, 1, 20.0, 20.089, 20.16, 1, 0, 0], [20, 1, 21.0, 21.089, 21.158, 1, 0, 0], [21, 1, 22.0, 22.093, 22.162, 1, 0, 0], [22, 1, 23.0, 23.095, 23.165, 1, 0, 0], [23, 1, 24.0, 24.092, 24.162, 1, 0, 0], [24, 1, 25.0, 25.099, 25.174, 1, 0, 0], [25, 1, 26.0, 26.093, 26.166, 1, 0, 0], [26, 1, 27.0, 27.102, 27.178, 1, 0, 0], [27, 1, 28.0, 28.098, 28.171, 1, 0, 0], [28, 1, 29.0, 29.095, 29.167, 1, 0, 0], [29, 1, 30.0, 30.094, 30.167, 1, 0, 0], [30, 1, 31.0, 31.1, 31.175, 